1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,009
Pantrix.

2
00:00:01,009 --> 00:00:04,570
A DevSecOps capstone project by Dhruv Sharma.

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:11,183
This video starts with the presentation and then walks through the running system.

4
00:00:12,583 --> 00:00:15,512
Part one: the presentation.

5
00:00:16,916 --> 00:00:22,142
Pantrix helps community food banks track donated stock and its expiry dates, so

6
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:24,955
food is handed out before it spoils.

7
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:30,180
It is built as a complete delivery pipeline, from a developer laptop to

8
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,788
a monitored Kubernetes cluster.

9
00:00:33,188 --> 00:00:39,624
Volunteers log donations, see what expires first, record hand-outs to partner organisations, and

10
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:42,594
the stock count goes down automatically.

11
00:00:42,594 --> 00:00:49,030
Expired stock cannot be handed out, and over-asking is rejected by the API.

12
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:56,217
These screenshots come from a real create, edit, hand-out and delete flow, captured

13
00:00:56,217 --> 00:01:01,113
against the running application, on desktop and on a phone-sized screen.

14
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:10,559
The stack follows a standard reference architecture: React with Vite, FastAPI, PostgreSQL with

15
00:01:10,559 --> 00:01:18,586
SQLAlchemy and Alembic, pytest, Docker, GitHub Actions, Trivy, the GitHub Container Registry, Terraform

16
00:01:18,586 --> 00:01:24,760
for AWS, Kubernetes with Helm, Argo CD, Prometheus and Grafana.

17
00:01:26,167 --> 00:01:28,969
Traffic enters through the ingress controller.

18
00:01:28,969 --> 00:01:35,040
Slash goes to the frontend Nginx pods, slash API goes to the FastAPI

19
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,374
pods, which talk to PostgreSQL.

20
00:01:37,374 --> 00:01:43,445
Prometheus scrapes every backend pod, Grafana shows the dashboard, and Argo CD keeps

21
00:01:43,445 --> 00:01:46,713
a second environment in sync with Git.

22
00:01:48,113 --> 00:01:54,968
Three tables, created by an Alembic migration: donors, stock items and distributions.

23
00:01:54,968 --> 00:02:02,395
The API offers full create, read, update and delete for items and donors,

24
00:02:02,395 --> 00:02:06,965
plus statistics, distributions, health, readiness and metrics endpoints.

25
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:16,781
Thirty-one backend tests reach ninety-six percent coverage against a throw-away database built by the real migrations.

26
00:02:16,781 --> 00:02:22,031
Ten frontend tests cover rendering, API failures and form validation.

27
00:02:22,031 --> 00:02:27,281
Ruff, Bandit, pip-audit, ESLint and npm audit run as well.

28
00:02:28,681 --> 00:02:30,785
Both images are multi-stage builds.

29
00:02:30,785 --> 00:02:36,254
The backend runs as user ten thousand and one, the frontend as user

30
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:39,620
one hundred and one on an unprivileged Nginx.

31
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:46,352
Docker Compose starts PostgreSQL, the API and the UI with health checks and a persistent volume.

32
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:51,113
The pipeline runs on every push to main.

33
00:02:51,113 --> 00:02:54,878
Tests, linting, secret scanning and infrastructure checks run first.

34
00:02:54,878 --> 00:03:00,315
Only then are both images built, checked for a non-root user, scanned by

35
00:03:00,315 --> 00:03:05,753
Trivy, and pushed to the GitHub Container Registry tagged with the commit SHA.

36
00:03:05,753 --> 00:03:10,772
A real run on GitHub is green, and part five shows it.

37
00:03:12,173 --> 00:03:13,833
Trivy found real problems.

38
00:03:13,833 --> 00:03:19,226
The first backend image failed the gate with four fixable high severity vulnerabilities

39
00:03:19,226 --> 00:03:23,375
in libraries vendored by pip, which the API never uses.

40
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:30,014
The fix removed pip from the runtime image, and the gate now passes without suppressing anything.

41
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:36,398
The Terraform describes a VPC with two public and two private subnets in

42
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:40,976
the Mumbai region and an EKS cluster with a managed node group.

43
00:03:40,976 --> 00:03:45,934
I applied it to a real AWS account, captured the Console and kubectl

44
00:03:45,934 --> 00:03:47,842
proof, and destroyed it again.

45
00:03:47,842 --> 00:03:50,130
Part six shows all of that.

46
00:03:51,539 --> 00:03:57,625
Helm deploys two replicas of each tier with hardened pods, an Ingress, and autoscalers.

47
00:03:57,625 --> 00:04:04,146
Under generated load, the horizontal pod autoscaler took the backend from two to six pods.

48
00:04:05,546 --> 00:04:07,919
Building and deploying are separate.

49
00:04:07,919 --> 00:04:11,715
Continuous integration only builds, scans and publishes images.

50
00:04:11,715 --> 00:04:17,885
Deployment is a Git commit that changes the image tag, and Argo CD

51
00:04:17,885 --> 00:04:20,257
reconciles the cluster to match.

52
00:04:21,657 --> 00:04:23,685
FastAPI exposes Prometheus metrics.

53
00:04:23,685 --> 00:04:30,276
Every backend pod is a target in state up, and the Grafana dashboard

54
00:04:30,276 --> 00:04:36,359
shows request rate, error ratio, latency percentiles, business events and pod resources.

55
00:04:37,759 --> 00:04:43,283
Five troubleshooting drills were scripted and run: a missing image tag, a Service

56
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:48,807
with no endpoints, a readiness failure, a migration failure, and a bad release

57
00:04:48,807 --> 00:04:51,356
that was rolled back without downtime.

58
00:04:51,356 --> 00:04:56,455
One drill exposed a real Nginx startup bug, which was then fixed.

59
00:04:57,873 --> 00:05:02,350
Everything in the middle and right columns is backed by real output in

60
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:07,515
the docs evidence folder, including the GitHub Actions run and the AWS apply and destroy.

61
00:05:08,915 --> 00:05:12,129
Part two: the working application.

62
00:05:13,529 --> 00:05:17,614
This is the live application through the Kubernetes Ingress.

63
00:05:17,614 --> 00:05:20,337
The shelf is sorted by expiry.

64
00:05:20,337 --> 00:05:27,144
A new donation is logged, edited, partly handed out to a shelter, and finally deleted.

65
00:05:27,144 --> 00:05:33,044
Every action calls the FastAPI backend, and the data is stored in PostgreSQL.

66
00:05:33,044 --> 00:05:36,675
The layout also adapts to a phone-sized screen.

67
00:05:56,929 --> 00:06:00,275
The same API is documented in Swagger.

68
00:06:00,275 --> 00:06:06,967
Here the statistics endpoint is executed live, followed by the raw Prometheus metrics endpoint.

69
00:06:12,729 --> 00:06:15,563
Part three: the codebase.

70
00:06:16,963 --> 00:06:25,716
The repository holds the backend, the frontend, the Helm chart, Kubernetes manifests, Terraform,

71
00:06:25,716 --> 00:06:34,470
GitOps desired state, monitoring, troubleshooting drills, scripts, workflows, documentation and the demo material.

72
00:06:35,870 --> 00:06:41,673
The distribution endpoint locks the stock row, rejects expired stock and over-asking with

73
00:06:41,673 --> 00:06:46,137
a conflict response, and decrements the quantity in one transaction.

74
00:06:47,537 --> 00:06:49,926
Liveness never touches the database.

75
00:06:49,926 --> 00:06:57,094
Readiness does, so a pod without a database stops receiving traffic but is not restarted.

76
00:06:57,094 --> 00:07:00,439
Prometheus instrumentation is added in one line.

77
00:07:01,839 --> 00:07:07,121
In the Helm chart, migrations run in an init container before the API

78
00:07:07,121 --> 00:07:10,777
starts, with an advisory lock so replicas never race.

79
00:07:10,777 --> 00:07:15,246
Every container runs as non-root with a read-only root file system.

80
00:07:16,646 --> 00:07:19,615
Part four: tests and Docker.

81
00:07:21,015 --> 00:07:24,979
Pytest runs thirty-one tests, all passing.

82
00:07:26,382 --> 00:07:34,005
The frontend passes ESLint, Prettier, ten Vitest tests, npm audit, and the production build.

83
00:07:35,415 --> 00:07:41,042
Docker Compose builds both images and waits until PostgreSQL, the backend and the

84
00:07:41,042 --> 00:07:42,773
frontend are all healthy.

85
00:07:44,182 --> 00:07:51,215
Both containers run as non-root users, confirmed from the image configuration and from the running process.

86
00:07:52,615 --> 00:07:56,673
Part five: continuous integration and delivery.

87
00:07:58,081 --> 00:08:01,451
This is the real job graph of the workflow.

88
00:08:01,451 --> 00:08:06,317
Images are only pushed after every gate passes, and they are tagged with

89
00:08:06,317 --> 00:08:08,189
the commit SHA, never latest.

90
00:08:09,589 --> 00:08:16,684
This is the real run on GitHub, run number six, triggered by a push to main.

91
00:08:16,684 --> 00:08:22,448
All seven jobs are green: backend, frontend, secret scan and infrastructure checks, then

92
00:08:22,448 --> 00:08:25,109
both images, then the kind deployment.

93
00:08:25,109 --> 00:08:31,760
The artifacts are the test reports, the Trivy reports and the software bills of materials.

94
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:39,548
In the backend job, pytest ran thirty-one tests, all passing, with ninety-six percent coverage.

95
00:08:39,548 --> 00:08:45,023
That is above the eighty-five percent floor that would fail the build.

96
00:08:46,427 --> 00:08:51,801
Trivy scans the backend image that was just built, tagged with the commit SHA.

97
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:56,790
A separate gate step fails the job on any fixable high or critical

98
00:08:56,790 --> 00:08:58,709
vulnerability, and this run passed.

99
00:09:00,109 --> 00:09:05,464
The push step publishes the image tagged with the full commit SHA, never

100
00:09:05,464 --> 00:09:07,523
latest, and prints the digest.

101
00:09:07,523 --> 00:09:10,407
A provenance attestation is attached to it.

102
00:09:11,809 --> 00:09:18,127
The backend package is public in the GitHub Container Registry, with tags that are commit SHAs.

103
00:09:19,542 --> 00:09:22,861
The frontend package is published the same way.

104
00:09:24,276 --> 00:09:29,118
A kind cluster then pulls those exact images from the registry, installs the

105
00:09:29,118 --> 00:09:32,843
Helm chart and runs a smoke test through the Ingress.

106
00:09:32,843 --> 00:09:35,078
It ends with smoke test passed.

107
00:09:36,479 --> 00:09:41,465
Finally the promote workflow ran and committed the verified commit SHA to the

108
00:09:41,465 --> 00:09:44,917
GitOps values file, which is what Argo CD deploys.

109
00:09:46,317 --> 00:09:51,037
The workflow files were validated with actionlint, with no problems found.

110
00:09:51,037 --> 00:09:54,470
Every action is pinned to a commit SHA.

111
00:09:55,883 --> 00:09:59,562
Part six: Terraform and AWS.

112
00:10:00,962 --> 00:10:04,402
This is the design described by the Terraform code.

113
00:10:04,402 --> 00:10:10,135
It was applied to a real AWS account in the Mumbai region and destroyed afterwards.

114
00:10:11,535 --> 00:10:15,214
Terraform initialises and validates successfully.

115
00:10:16,614 --> 00:10:21,293
The plan against the real account resolves sixty-two resources to add.

116
00:10:22,693 --> 00:10:27,759
The first apply created fifty-eight resources and then stopped at the node group.

117
00:10:27,759 --> 00:10:32,824
The account is on the AWS Free plan, which rejects the t3 medium

118
00:10:32,824 --> 00:10:37,110
instance type, so the autoscaling group could not launch any nodes.

119
00:10:38,527 --> 00:10:43,957
With a Free plan eligible instance type, m7i flex large, the second apply

120
00:10:43,957 --> 00:10:47,299
replaced the node group and finished without errors.

121
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:54,049
In the AWS Console, the VPC pantrix dev exists in the Mumbai region,

122
00:10:54,049 --> 00:10:59,397
with the ten dot forty-two dot zero dot zero slash sixteen address range.

123
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:05,703
It has two public and two private subnets across two availability zones.

124
00:11:07,104 --> 00:11:12,501
The EKS cluster pantrix dev is active on Kubernetes one point thirty-five, with

125
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:16,237
two nodes, one node group and zero health issues.

126
00:11:17,637 --> 00:11:22,641
The managed node group general is active, with a desired size of two.

127
00:11:24,041 --> 00:11:30,447
kubectl against the cluster shows both nodes Ready, and the system pods, including

128
00:11:30,447 --> 00:11:34,389
CoreDNS, kube-proxy and the network plugin, are running.

129
00:11:35,789 --> 00:11:41,321
Finally terraform destroy removed all sixty-two resources, and a check of the account

130
00:11:41,321 --> 00:11:45,577
found nothing named pantrix left, so no cost is running.

131
00:11:46,989 --> 00:11:52,277
Part seven: Kubernetes, Helm, the Ingress and Argo CD.

132
00:11:53,689 --> 00:12:00,737
The Helm release runs two backend pods, two frontend pods and PostgreSQL, all running.

133
00:12:02,137 --> 00:12:09,055
ClusterIP services, the Ingress for pantrix dot localtest dot me, and the autoscalers.

134
00:12:10,470 --> 00:12:15,820
Helm shows the release history, including a failed bad release and its rollback,

135
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:17,878
and the Helm test passes.

136
00:12:19,278 --> 00:12:24,214
Under load, CPU reached almost four hundred percent of the target and the

137
00:12:24,214 --> 00:12:27,631
autoscaler scaled the backend from two to six pods.

138
00:12:29,045 --> 00:12:33,761
Argo CD shows the dev application synced and healthy, deployed from a Git

139
00:12:33,761 --> 00:12:35,938
commit that sets the image SHA.

140
00:12:49,378 --> 00:12:54,472
Every promotion is a commit to the GitOps values file, and Argo CD

141
00:12:54,472 --> 00:12:56,431
reconciles the cluster to it.

142
00:12:57,845 --> 00:13:00,379
Part eight: monitoring.

143
00:13:01,779 --> 00:13:07,111
Prometheus shows every backend pod as a target in state up, a live

144
00:13:07,111 --> 00:13:09,982
request-rate query, and the Pantrix alert rules.

145
00:13:16,259 --> 00:13:22,374
The Grafana dashboard updates live with request rate, error ratio, latency percentiles, stock

146
00:13:22,374 --> 00:13:25,197
events, and pod CPU and memory.

147
00:13:39,299 --> 00:13:42,133
Part nine: troubleshooting.

148
00:13:43,533 --> 00:13:49,525
A Service whose selector matches no pods has no endpoints, so requests are refused.

149
00:13:49,525 --> 00:13:55,945
Comparing the selector with the pod labels finds the cause, and correcting it restores traffic.

150
00:13:57,345 --> 00:14:02,681
A release with a missing image fails, but the old pods keep serving,

151
00:14:02,681 --> 00:14:04,733
so every request still succeeds.

152
00:14:04,733 --> 00:14:08,017
Helm rollback returns to the last good revision.

153
00:14:09,418 --> 00:14:10,732
That is Pantrix.

154
00:14:10,732 --> 00:14:15,549
Real output for every claim is in the docs evidence folder.

155
00:14:15,549 --> 00:14:17,301
Thank you for watching.

